  "assumptions": [
   "normal incidence: one incident mode, one polarisation",
   "single homogeneous layer of finite real thickness h",
   "both half-spaces semi-infinite with REAL characteristic impedance Z1, Z2 (no radiation leaking out of the problem)",
   "planar, infinite, time-invariant interfaces",
   "material parameters frequency-independent over the band",
   "the matching element is series, lumped and non-dispersive"
  ],
  "theorem": "For real Zm, Z2 the input impedance obeys\n  Re Zin = Z2 Zm^2/(Z2^2 sin^2 + Zm^2 cos^2),\n  Im Zin = Zm(Zm^2 - Z2^2) sin cos/(Z2^2 sin^2 + Zm^2 cos^2),\nwith theta = k_m h.  Consequently Im Zin vanishes only at theta = m pi/2, where Re Zin equals Z2 (m even) or Zm^2/Z2 (m odd).  The set of REAL values Zin attains is therefore exactly {Z2, Zm^2/Z2} when Zm != Z2, and {Z2} identically when Zm = Z2.  Hence for real lossless media, exact zero reflection occurs iff Z1 = Z2 (trivial) or Zm^2 = Z1 Z2 with k_m h = (2n+1) pi/2.",
  "note": "This is a complete classification for the one-layer scalar problem, and it is the honest version of the quarter-wave result: the quarter-wave condition is the UNIQUE nontrivial reflectionless configuration, not merely a sufficient one.  The earlier audit's claim that 'only gain can close the floor with loss' is REJECTED as a general statement: it holds only for the one-knob family where the interphase material is fixed.  With the interphase impedance free, Re Zin = Z1 is attainable with loss, and one lossless reactance then gives exact zero reflection WITH absorption present.  Note also that in this model a lossy layer is NOT described by a complex Zm alone: attenuation inside the layer makes Zin depend on h through a complex k_m h, so the two-knob count above is an idealisation that must be checked case by case."
 },
