  "note": "(i) Fourier limit (tau = 0): the thermal root is purely diffusive for every material at every frequency; |k_th| ~ 1/delta with delta sub-micron at MHz, so no thermal branch propagates.  That part of the earlier audit stands where it applies: it applies to CLASSICAL thermoelasticity.  (ii) In Lord-Shulman the root acquires a real part once omega*tau > 1 and then propagates at v_T = sqrt(K/(rho c_e tau)).  The earlier audit's 'the thermal field is inert' is therefore OVERTURNED as a general statement: it is a statement about tau, not about piezothermoelasticity.  (iii) The corrective result is a CONSTRAINT, not a verdict: v_T/c_L = sqrt(2 pi f_on tau_min) with tau_min = kappa/c_L^2, so the thermal wave speed and its onset frequency cannot be chosen independently.  Choosing tau to place the onset inside an ultrasonic band drives v_T/c_L down in proportion, and choosing tau for a usable v_T pushes the onset into the GHz range.  THIS is the defensible statement a paper can make."
 },
