  "mode_count": "Exact general solution per medium: w = a+ e^{i kappa z} + a- e^{-i kappa z} and psi = p+ e^{k z} + p- e^{-k z}. Four amplitudes against four conditions (w, phi, sigma_z3, D_z): balanced, and only ONE propagating branch (the kappa pair).  The psi pair is the evanescent gauge partner and degenerates at k = 0, where the 4x4 system is singular - normal incidence must be taken as a limit.",
  "thermal_decoupling": "The temperature field decouples EXACTLY in this reduction: for 6mm symmetry with alpha = (a1, a1, a3), beta_13 = beta_23 = 0, and invariance along the poling direction x3 removes beta_3j theta_,j.  So an SH problem cannot test thermal coupling at all.",
  "spring_incomplete": "A purely tangential spring in SH leaves four amplitudes against only three independent conditions (traction jump law, phi law, D law), so the interface law set is incomplete unless w-continuity is retained - in which case the spring has nothing to act on.  Spring interfaces therefore need the P-SV problem to be meaningful.",
  "retraction": "RETRACTION of this audit's own intermediate finding.  An earlier version reported an exact reflection zero at k = 2890.43 for this system.  That came from two compounding errors: kappa^2 had the wrong sign (giving a regime with no propagating branch at all), and the sigma_z3 and D_z rows used kappa where the dpsi term belongs.  With both corrected, |R| is STRICTLY INCREASING in k and the minimum is the bulk mismatch at k -> 0: there is no interference minimum to find.",
  "consequence": "Two positive results come out of the negative one.  (1) The elastic (non-piezoelectric) SH junction DOES have a genuine minimum (|R| ~ 1e-6 at k = 1730), while the piezoelectric one does not: piezoelectric coupling DESTROYS the elastic SH interference minimum rather than creating one.  (2) Because the minimum exists only in the elastic case and vanishes when e15 != 0 on either side, any reflection-minimum claim in a piezoelectric junction must be a P-SV (in-plane) phenomenon, where two polarisations can interfere.  This is a structural constraint on the blueprint, not an opinion."
 },
