  "statement": "For a passive linear time-invariant junction, R_E + T_E + A_E = 1 with A_E >= 0, and A_E = 0 iff every material in the finite region is lossless.  Hence (a) R_E -> 0 with A_E > 0 is allowed, so reflectionlessness does NOT imply perfect transmission; and (b) R_E = 0 together with T_E = 1 forces A_E = 0, i.e. requires a lossless junction.  Exact reflectionless FULL transmission is therefore a losslessness statement, not a matching statement.",
  "measured_behaviour": "In the matched layer the reflected power is second order in the loss (R_E ~ 1e-8 at eta = 1e-4) because the first-order effect of loss is not a reflection but a change of the wave amplitude through the layer.  The transmitted power loses FIRST order: T_E = 1 - 3.5e-4 at eta = 1e-4 against T_E = 1.000000 when lossless.  So a lossy interphase is a nearly reflectionless but noticeably attenuating window, and the two figures of merit diverge at first order in the loss while the reflection figure barely moves.  An optimisation that reports only R_E would class this design as excellent.",
